Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program that uses some advanced features like async/await, Promises, destructuring, template literals, and the spread operator in a complex way:

```javascript
const fetchData = async (url) => {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
    return response.json();
};

const processData = (data) => {
    const { id, title, ...otherProps } = data;
    return { id, title: title.toUpperCase(), ...otherProps, processed: true };
};

const fetchAndProcessData = async (url) => {
    try {
        const data = await fetchData(url);
        return processData(data);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Error fetching data: ${error.message}`);
    }
};

const urls = [
    'https: 
    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/2',
];

const main = async () => {
    const results = await Promise.all(urls.map(async (url) => {
        const result = await fetchAndProcessData(url);
        return result ? `Processed Data: ${JSON.stringify(result)}` : 'Failed to process';
    }));
    results.forEach(console.log);
};

main();
```

This program fetches data from a couple of URLs, processes the data by transforming it, and outputs the results, demonstrating the use of several JavaScript features.